      // ...but a string with NO interpolation is a literal, and desugaring it
      // to `("" + "a")` made it a computed value: `--enable-frozen-string-literal`
      // never froze anything, because the freeze is applied to EStrL and a
      // double-quoted string never was one. lex_dstr_t returns exactly two
      // tokens when it read no `#{}`, which is the shape folded here.
      // ⚠ ...and in a file whose magic comment is not UTF-8, a \u escape
      //   for a non-ASCII character makes the literal UTF-8 (the escape has
      //   no other way to be spelled), where every other literal takes the
      //   file's encoding. The lexer is the only one that sees the escape,
      //   so it says so with the closer: `(s ... )u8` (see the "(s" parse).
      if lex_u8_escaped s (i + 1) (j - 1) then
        // (...which cannot sit beside the file's own non-ASCII characters:
        //  "UTF-8 mixed within EUC-JP source")
